/** Retrofit batch AX -- upgrading old 2-case (and, for two problems, 3-case) gpe-history problems to
 * the 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress). All solvers below were independently
 * written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's real official Sample before being trusted
 * to generate new tricky/stress expected output.
 *
 * gpe-2009-02-line-overlap-problem: rather than the naive O(n^2) all-pairs overlap sum, this uses the
 * identity that the total pairwise-overlap length equals the integral over x of C(coverage(x), 2) --
 * i.e. a coordinate sweep over start(+1)/end(-1) events, adding width*C(activeCount,2) between
 * consecutive event x-coordinates. BigInt throughout since the result can approach 2^63.
 * gpe-2009-17-binary-tree-traversals: classic preorder+inorder -> postorder reconstruction via
 *   divide-and-conquer on inorder-index ranges.
 * gpe-2009-24-unique-lines: canonicalize each pair's line as a reduced (dy,-dx,c) normal-form triple
 *   (direction reduced by gcd with a consistent sign convention) and count distinct triples in a Set.
 * gpe-2015-01-missing-numbers: XOR of all elements in list i together with all elements in list i+1
 *   isolates the one removed value, and this stays correct even with duplicate values elsewhere in
 *   the list (removing exactly one instance of a value always flips the running XOR by that value).
 * gpe-2015-02-recursion-and-mod: f(n)+2 = 3*(f(n-1)+2) makes f(n)+2 geometric with ratio 3 and
 *   f(1)+2=3, so f(n) = 3^n - 2 in closed form; computed via BigInt fast modular exponentiation since
 *   n can be up to 2^63-1.
 * gpe-2015-03-sudoku-as-good-as-lee-hsien-loong: standard MRV-heuristic backtracking solver with
 *   row/col/box bitmasks. NOTE: the real official Sample for this problem was independently found to
 *   have AT LEAST 2 valid solutions (verified via an exhaustive solution-counting pass, and confirmed
 *   both this solver's alternate solution and the scraped official one satisfy every given clue and
 *   all row/column/box constraints) despite the problem statement's explicit claim of uniqueness -- a
 *   pre-existing data-quality issue in the original problem, not a bug in this solver. This is also the
 *   third strict-checker/multi-answer caveat found this session (after gpe-10658 in batch AU and
 *   gpe-10906 in batch AV). To avoid seeding data with the same ambiguity, every boundary/tricky/stress
 *   puzzle generated here was constructed via cell-by-cell removal from a full random grid with an
 *   exhaustive uniqueness check (capped at 2 solutions) after each removal, guaranteeing a genuinely
 *   unique solution.
 * gpe-2015-04-the-n-th-element: binary search on the ANSWER VALUE V (not the merged array, which would
 *   be far too large to materialize at N=10^7): for a candidate V, count how many of each quadratic
 *   sequence's first N terms are <=V via an inner binary search on the index (both sequences are
 *   strictly increasing), and find the smallest V where the combined count reaches N. All arithmetic in
 *   BigInt since intermediate values can reach the stated 64-bit range.
 */
